course: dictionary
lesson: 3916
title: The froghopper
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A froghopper is a small insect that is a very strong jumper for its size. Its name makes you think of a frog, and it does jump, but it is an insect, not a frog.
explain: Read the context: «An adult froghopper can leap about 70 centimetres up.» Seventy centimetres is a long way for such a small creature, so the froghopper is one of the best jumpers on Earth for its size.
explain: Now look at the clue «cuckoo spit». You might expect spit from a cuckoo, but it is not. The white froth on plants in spring is made by froghopper young, who blow it from their own sticky liquid to hide and keep moist.
fact: A froghopper is a small jumping insect.
fact: Froghopper young live inside a blob of froth called cuckoo spit.
fact: Cuckoo spit is not spit from cuckoos; the froth is made by the young froghoppers.
fact: An adult froghopper can leap about 70 centimetres, so it is one of the best jumpers on Earth for its size.
fact: A froghopper is also called a spittlebug.
exercise: What is a froghopper? | *A small jumping insect whose young live in froth on plants | A bird that sings in spring | A frog that lives in ponds
exercise: «An adult froghopper can leap about 70 centimetres up.» How far can it leap? | About 7 metres | About 700 metres | *About 70 centimetres | About 7 centimetres
exercise: What is the white froth on plants in spring called? | Plant milk | Spring snow | *Cuckoo spit | Frog foam
test: Why is the froth called cuckoo spit if it is not spit from cuckoos? | *It is made by froghopper young, who blow it from their own sticky liquid | The froth comes from rain on the leaves | It is made by a plant called cuckoo | Cuckoos spit on plants in spring
test: What other name does the froghopper have? | *Spittlebug | Leaper | Froghopper jumper | Cuckoo
exam: In «its young hide in froth they blow from their own sticky liquid», what is the froth used for? | Food for birds | To carry the young to new plants | To make the plant grow | *To hide the young and keep them moist
